<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Opava</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Troppau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Opava (German: Troppau) is a city in the Moravian-Silesian Region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, on the Opava river. It was the seat of the Duchy of Opava, ruled by a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a>, and from 1742 the capital of Austrian Silesia. In 1820 it hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-troppau">Congress of Troppau</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-alliance">Holy Alliance</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/opava.jpg" alt="Opava" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MOs810 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horn%C3%AD_n%C3%A1m%C4%9Bst%C3%AD,_Opawa,_Opava-M%C4%9Bsto_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Opava lies on the Opava River, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and about 27 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrava">Ostrava</a>. The river takes its name from old Celtic words for water, and the town takes its name from the river, in German as Troppau, from zu Oppa, meaning on the Oppa, and in Polish as Opawa. Most of its territory lies in the Opava Hilly Land within the Silesian Lowlands, and the Opava and Moravice rivers meet within it. It is one of the historical centres of Silesia and was a historical capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-silesia">Czech Silesia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town is first mentioned in writing in 1195 and received town privileges in 1224. In 1269 the Duchy of Troppau was established with the town as its capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a> ruled the duchy from 1427 to 1431, and in 1485 it was acquired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> and ruled by Hungarians until 1526. In 1613 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-i-prince-of-liechtenstein">Karl I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a> became Duke of Troppau and merged the duchy with the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krnov">Krnov</a>. The title of Duke of Troppau continues in the Liechtenstein family.</p><p>Most of Silesia was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a>, after 1740. The part that remained with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy became known as Austrian Silesia, with its capital at Troppau from 1742 to 1918. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-troppau">Congress of Troppau</a> met there from 24 October to 23 December 1820. By the Austrian census of 1910 the town had 30,762 inhabitants, of whom 92 percent gave German as their native language, 6.9 percent Czech and 0.9 percent Polish.</p><p>In 1919 the town became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, whose government established the name Opava. It was ceded to Nazi Germany in 1938 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a> and administered as part of Reichsgau Sudetenland. The Soviet Red Army occupied it on 22 April 1945, at the cost of enormous war damage. In 1945 and 1946 the German population was expelled under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benes-decrees">Beneš decrees</a> and the city was resettled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Opava is a regional centre of education and culture. The Silesian University was established in 1991, and the Silesian Museum, founded in 1814, is the oldest public museum in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>. The Silesian Theatre dates from 1805. The city's two main landmarks are the city hall tower, known as Hláska, built in 1614 to 1618, and the Co-Cathedral of the Assumption of the Virgin Mary, the largest building in the Czech Republic in Silesian Brick Gothic style, whose southern tower, 102 metres high, is the tallest in Silesia. The Chapel of the Holy Cross, from 1394, is also a national cultural monument.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Opava” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1269 King Ottokar II of Bohemia gave the <b>Opava</b> region of northern Moravia to his illegitimate son Nicholas I. Nicholas's descendants ruled it as a separate duchy, and it gradually came to be counted among the Silesian duchies rather than Moravia, under the Kingdom of Bohemia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-opava">Duchy of Opava</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Opava</b> Přemyslids ruled Racibórz as part of a combined duchy of Opava and Racibórz, later separate.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-raciborz">Duchy of Racibórz</a></li><li><blockquote>Prussia seized most of Silesia in the War of the Austrian Succession, and under the Treaty of Breslau and the Peace of Berlin in 1742 Austria kept only a southern strip, including the duchies of <b>Troppau</b>, Teschen and part of Jägerndorf.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-upper-and-lower-silesia">Duchy of Upper and Lower Silesia</a></li></ul></section>
